# modules/iac/dockerfile.rules - docs/DESIGN.md §8.2's Dockerfile catalog
# (docs/DESIGN.md §13 step 4, "IaC (cloud + container rules)... reuse the
# rule engine"), this ticket.
#
# Path note: same discipline modules/iac/terraform.rules documents at its own
# top - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's frozen path table grants the pattern-rule
# schema to `modules/iac/*.rules` FLAT, so this pack lives at
# modules/iac/dockerfile.rules, never modules/iac/rules/dockerfile.rules or
# modules/iac/checks.rules (the latter basename is reserved repository-wide
# for the §9.5 script-check schema).
#
# Scope discipline, per this ticket's own text: `files:` below is
# deliberately the three literal glob forms `Dockerfile`, `Dockerfile.*`, and
# `*.dockerfile` - nothing else. docs/DESIGN.md §3's original layout sketch
# grouped "Dockerfile, Kubernetes, docker-compose, Helm" into one
# `containers.rules` file; modules/iac/terraform.rules already established
# that FOUNDATION's actual build splits the grouped §3 sketch into one
# vendored pack per format under modules/iac/ (terraform.rules landed alone,
# not a combined cloud.rules), and this ticket continues that split for the
# container side: docker-compose and Helm values are explicitly OUT of scope
# here and left to a separate ticket, so this pack's `files:` list must never
# grow to cover `docker-compose*.yml` or a Helm `values.yaml` without that
# separate ticket doing it deliberately.  Nothing needs an `exclude-files`
# entry to keep those out: `files:` is an allow-list (OR, §9.1.2), so a path
# that matches none of the three globs never reaches the engine at all -
# tests/suites/iac.sh's docker-compose.yml/values.yaml fixtures pin exactly
# that reading.
#
# Six seed rules, straight out of docs/DESIGN.md §8.2's own list, in the same
# order it states them: runs as root / no USER, unpinned :latest base tag,
# secrets in ENV/ARG, ADD from a URL, curl|sh build steps, no pinned base
# digest.
#
# IAC-DOCKER-ROOT_USER-01 and IAC-DOCKER-UNPINNED_DIGEST-01 are both ABSENCE
# checks on the same `^FROM\s+\S+` trigger line (the same shape
# IAC-TF-KEY_ROTATION_DISABLED-01 and SAST-JAVA-XXE_DISALLOW_DOCTYPE-01
# already establish for this engine): a `context-deny` with a non-zero (or,
# for UNPINNED_DIGEST, zero) window is the only way §8's lookahead-free ERE
# subset can express "unless X appears nearby", so both carry the reviewed
# W033 advisory §10.2 describes rather than a lookahead.  ROOT_USER's window
# is 50 - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §10's E032 ceiling - because the guarding
# `USER` instruction can legitimately sit anywhere later in a short
# Dockerfile and a narrower window would just manufacture false positives on
# every fixture that happens to declare a few RUN/COPY steps first.
# UNPINNED_DIGEST's `@sha256:` deny is a same-line-intent guard exactly like
# `SAST-SEC-AWS_AKID-01`'s own EXAMPLE/placeholder deny, so it stays at
# `context-window: 0`.
#
# Known trade-off, honestly stated rather than silently accepted: this
# engine has no concept of a Dockerfile "stage" (a multi-stage `FROM
# builder AS runtime` referencing an earlier stage by name, not a real
# registry image). `^FROM\s+\S+` matches a stage-alias FROM line exactly
# like a real base-image FROM line, so UNPINNED_DIGEST-01 (and, for a stage
# alias that happens to end in a literal ":latest" segment, LATEST_TAG-01)
# can fire on an internal stage reference that was never pulled from a
# registry and so was never a supply-chain concern. Tightening this needs
# stage-name tracking the engine does not have; deferred rather than solved
# with a lookahead the §8.2 ERE subset forbids.

id: IAC-DOCKER-ROOT_USER-01
title: Dockerfile builds an image with no non-root USER instruction
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-250
owasp: A05:2021
pattern: ^FROM\s+\S+
dialect: ere
files: Dockerfile
files: Dockerfile.*
files: *.dockerfile
context-deny: ^\s*USER\s+
context-window: 50
tags: static
tags: compliance
remediation: Create an unprivileged account (`RUN addgroup -S app && adduser -S app -G app` or
  equivalent for the base distro) and add a `USER app` instruction before the container's default
  entrypoint/cmd runs, so the process does not run as root by default. An attacker who achieves code
  execution inside the container then does not automatically inherit root privileges in the
  container's own namespace.
references: CWE-250

id: IAC-DOCKER-LATEST_TAG-01
title: Base image pinned to the mutable :latest tag
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-829
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: ^FROM\s+\S+:latest\b
dialect: ere
files: Dockerfile
files: Dockerfile.*
files: *.dockerfile
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Pin the base image to an explicit, immutable release tag (and ideally an
  `@sha256:...` digest, see IAC-DOCKER-UNPINNED_DIGEST-01) rather than `:latest`, so a rebuild does
  not silently pull in an unreviewed upstream change.
references: CWE-829

id: IAC-DOCKER-SECRET_ENV-01
title: Credential-shaped literal assigned via a Dockerfile ENV or ARG instruction
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-798
owasp: A07:2021
pattern: ^(ENV|ARG)\s+[A-Za-z0-9_]*(PASSWORD|SECRET|TOKEN|API_KEY|ACCESS_KEY|PRIVATE_KEY)[A-Za-z0-9_]*\s*=\s*\S+
dialect: ere
files: Dockerfile
files: Dockerfile.*
files: *.dockerfile
context-deny: (CHANGEME|changeme|EXAMPLE|example|XXXXXXXX|xxxxxxxx|placeholder|dummy|TODO)
context-window: 0
tags: static
remediation: Remove the literal from the Dockerfile and rotate it immediately - both ENV and ARG
  values are baked into the image's layer history and are recoverable with `docker history` even
  from a later layer that unsets them. Inject the secret at container-start time instead (an
  orchestrator secret store, a mounted file, or `--env-file` kept out of the image), or use a
  multi-stage build's `--mount=type=secret` build-time-only mount for a value the build itself needs.
references: CWE-798

id: IAC-DOCKER-REMOTE_ADD-01
title: ADD instruction fetches a remote URL directly into the image
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-494
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: ^ADD\s+\S*https?://
dialect: ere
files: Dockerfile
files: Dockerfile.*
files: *.dockerfile
tags: static
remediation: Use COPY for anything already in the build context. For a remote artifact, replace
  ADD with an explicit RUN step that fetches it (curl/wget) and verifies a checksum or signature
  before extracting it, rather than relying on ADD's implicit, unauthenticated fetch-and-extract.
references: CWE-494

id: IAC-DOCKER-PIPE_TO_SHELL-01
title: Build step pipes a downloaded script straight into a shell
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-494
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: ^RUN\s+.*(curl|wget)\s+[^|]*\|\s*(sudo\s+)?(sh|bash|zsh|ash)\b
dialect: ere
files: Dockerfile
files: Dockerfile.*
files: *.dockerfile
tags: static
remediation: Download the script to a file, verify its checksum or GPG signature, then execute the
  verified file, instead of piping an unauthenticated remote script straight into a shell - a
  compromised or MITM'd download otherwise runs arbitrary code in the build with no review step.
references: CWE-494

id: IAC-DOCKER-UNPINNED_DIGEST-01
title: Base image not pinned to a content digest
severity: low
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-829
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: ^FROM\s+\S+
dialect: ere
files: Dockerfile
files: Dockerfile.*
files: *.dockerfile
context-deny: @sha256:
context-window: 0
tags: static
remediation: Pin the base image with an `@sha256:<digest>` suffix (`docker pull` and then
  `docker inspect --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}'` the image you intend to use, or read the
  digest off the registry) in addition to, or instead of, a tag - a tag can be repointed at a
  different image server-side, but a digest cannot.
references: CWE-829
